---
/**
 * Redistricting 2025 Page — Covers the mid-cycle congressional redistricting
 * effort led by Missouri's Republican legislature and signed by Gov. Kehoe
 * in September 2025. Includes side-by-side map comparison, legal analysis,
 * impact assessment, and current status of court challenges and referendum.
 *
 * This page aims for balanced, nonpartisan coverage — presenting both sides'
 * legal arguments and factual impacts without editorial judgment.
 */

import BaseLayout from '../components/layout/BaseLayout.astro';
import RedistrictingComparison from '../components/maps/RedistrictingComparison.svelte';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="2025 Mid-Cycle Redistricting"
  description="Missouri's 2025 congressional redistricting: side-by-side map comparison, legal arguments, community impact, the Proposition A referendum, and the ongoing state and federal court fight over which map is used in November 2026."
>
  <!-- Page header -->
  <section class="bg-mo-navy text-white py-10">
    <div class="container-site">
      <h1 class="font-headline text-3xl font-bold mb-2">2025 Mid-Cycle Redistricting</h1>
      <p class="text-white/70 max-w-2xl">
        In September 2025, Missouri's legislature redrew congressional district boundaries
        outside the normal post-census cycle. Here's what happened, why it matters, and
        where things stand.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Current status callout — the legal fight is moving daily, so the as-of date is explicit -->
  <section class="container-site pt-8" aria-labelledby="status-heading">
    <div class="max-w-3xl p-5 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-mo-gold bg-mo-gold/10">
      <h2 id="status-heading" class="font-headline text-lg font-bold text-mo-neutral-800 mb-2">
        Status: Which map is in effect? Still unsettled.
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        As of <strong>September 23, 2026</strong>, state and federal courts disagree on
        which map Missouri should use in the <strong>November 3, 2026</strong> general election.
        The Missouri Supreme Court ruled that the voter referendum suspends the 2025 map and
        ordered the 2022 map to be used. The U.S. Supreme Court has twice declined to disturb
        that result. On September 21, the 8th U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals ruled the other way
        and ordered use of the 2025 map, the map used in the August primary. That ruling is on hold until 5 p.m.
        September 28, and the challengers have asked the U.S. Supreme Court to step in a
        third time. Absentee ballots using the 2022 districts are already going out.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-700 leading-relaxed mt-2">
        Separately, voters will decide the map's permanent fate on
        <strong>Proposition A</strong> this November. <a href="#where-it-stands" class="text-mo-navy underline hover:text-mo-navy-light">Full details below.</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What Happened — Timeline -->
  <section class="container-site py-8">
    <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-mo-neutral-800 mb-4">What Happened</h2>

    <div class="prose prose-sm max-w-3xl text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        On August 29, 2025, Gov. Mike Kehoe called a special session of the Missouri General
        Assembly to redraw the state's eight congressional districts. The effort was part of a
        broader national push — coordinated by the White House and the National Republican
        Redistricting Trust — to redraw maps in several states ahead of the 2026 midterms to
        protect the Republican House majority.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Missouri House passed HB 1 on September 9 (90-65, party-line), the Senate followed
        on September 12 (21-11), and Gov. Kehoe signed it into law on September 28, 2025.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Timeline -->
    <div class="mt-6 border-l-2 border-mo-navy/20 pl-6 space-y-6 max-w-2xl">
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-mo-navy font-bold uppercase">Aug 29, 2025</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-700 font-semibold">Special session called</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-500">Gov. Kehoe announces special session for congressional redistricting and initiative petition reform.</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-mo-navy font-bold uppercase">Sept 3, 2025</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-700 font-semibold">Special session convenes</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-500">Legislature begins work on HB 1, sponsored by Rep. Dirk Deaton (House) and Sen. Rusty Black (Senate).</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-mo-navy font-bold uppercase">Sept 9, 2025</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-700 font-semibold">House passes HB 1</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-500">Party-line vote, 90-65. All Republican ayes, all Democratic nays.</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-mo-navy font-bold uppercase">Sept 12, 2025</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-700 font-semibold">Senate passes HB 1</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-500">Vote 21-11. Map heads to Gov. Kehoe's desk.</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-mo-navy font-bold uppercase">Sept 28, 2025</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-700 font-semibold">Governor signs HB 1</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-500">New districts become law. Legal challenges and referendum efforts begin.</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-mo-navy font-bold uppercase">Dec 2025</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-700 font-semibold">Referendum signatures submitted</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-500">People Not Politicians submits about 305,000 signatures to put the map to a statewide vote.</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-mo-navy font-bold uppercase">Mar 24, 2026</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-700 font-semibold">Missouri Supreme Court allows mid-decade redistricting</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-500"><em>Luther v. Hoskins</em>, 4-3: the state constitution requires redistricting after a census but does not prohibit it at other times.</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-mo-navy font-bold uppercase">Aug 4, 2026</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-700 font-semibold">Primary held under the 2025 map</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-500">The referendum had not been certified, so state officials ran the primary with the new districts.</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-mo-navy font-bold uppercase">Aug 2026</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-700 font-semibold">Cole County judges side with the Secretary of State</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-500">Judge Christopher Limbaugh upholds rejecting about 103,000 signatures gathered before Oct. 14, 2025. On Aug. 19, Judge Daniel Green rules that congressional maps cannot be put to a referendum at all.</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-mo-navy font-bold uppercase">Sept 3, 2026</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-700 font-semibold">Missouri Supreme Court orders a referendum vote</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-500"><em>von Glahn v. Hoskins</em>, unanimous: the referendum is valid, the 2025 map is suspended, and the 2022 map applies in November.</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-mo-navy font-bold uppercase">Sept 8, 2026</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-700 font-semibold">Dueling orders</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-500">Justice Kavanaugh denies the Secretary of State's request to pause the state ruling. The same day, U.S. District Judge Stephen Clark orders use of the 2025 map.</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-mo-navy font-bold uppercase">Sept 10, 2026</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-700 font-semibold">U.S. Supreme Court blocks the federal order</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-500">An unsigned order, with no noted dissents, stays Judge Clark's order and keeps the 2022 map in place.</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-mo-navy font-bold uppercase">Sept 21, 2026</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-700 font-semibold">8th Circuit orders the 2025 map</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-500">The appeals court holds that switching maps between the primary and general election is unconstitutional. The ruling is paused until Sept. 28.</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-mo-navy font-bold uppercase">Sept 22, 2026</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-700 font-semibold">Back to the U.S. Supreme Court</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-500">Challengers ask the justices to block the 8th Circuit ruling. This is the dispute's third trip to the Court in September.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Side-by-side map comparison -->
  <section class="container-site py-8">
    <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-mo-neutral-800 mb-2">The Maps: Before and After</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 mb-4 max-w-3xl">
      Pan and zoom either map — both stay in sync. Click a district for population
      and demographic breakdown.
    </p>

    <div class="rounded-lg border border-mo-neutral-200 bg-white shadow-sm overflow-hidden">
      <div class="h-[400px] md:h-[550px]">
        <RedistrictingComparison client:load />
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Key changes table -->
    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-mo-neutral-100 text-left">
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-mo-neutral-700" scope="col">District</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-mo-neutral-700" scope="col">2022 Map</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-mo-neutral-700" scope="col">HB 1 (2025)</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-mo-neutral-700" scope="col">Partisan Shift</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200">
          <tr>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">CD-1</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-mo-neutral-600">St. Louis (safe D)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-mo-neutral-600">Minimal changes</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2"><span class="text-blue-600 font-medium">Stays D</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-gray-50">
            <td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">CD-2</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-mo-neutral-600">St. Louis suburbs (R+11)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-mo-neutral-600">Extended into southern MO</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2"><span class="text-red-600 font-medium">R+11 → R+14</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">CD-3</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-mo-neutral-600">Central/northeast MO</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-mo-neutral-600">More compact, St. Charles to Hannibal</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2"><span class="text-red-600 font-medium">Stays R+20</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-gray-50">
            <td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">CD-4</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-mo-neutral-600">West-central MO (safe R)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-mo-neutral-600">Gains part of Kansas City (west of Troost)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2"><span class="text-red-600 font-medium">More safely R</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-yellow-50">
            <td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">CD-5</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-mo-neutral-600">Kansas City (safe D, Rep. Cleaver)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-mo-neutral-600 font-medium">KC remnant + 14 rural counties east to Osage County</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2"><span class="font-bold">Solid D → <span class="text-red-600">Solid R</span></span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-gray-50">
            <td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">CD-6</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-mo-neutral-600">Northwest MO (safe R)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-mo-neutral-600">Gains part of Kansas City</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2"><span class="text-red-600 font-medium">Stays safely R</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">CD-7</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-mo-neutral-600">Southwest MO (safe R)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-mo-neutral-600">No significant change</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2"><span class="text-red-600 font-medium">No change</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-gray-50">
            <td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">CD-8</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-mo-neutral-600">Southeast MO (safe R)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-mo-neutral-600">No significant change</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2"><span class="text-red-600 font-medium">No change</span></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-mo-neutral-500 italic">
      Net effect: Projected delegation shifts from 6R-2D to 7R-1D. CD-5 is the primary target.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Community Impact -->
  <section class="container-site py-8">
    <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-mo-neutral-800 mb-4">Impact on Communities</h2>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
      <div class="p-5 rounded-lg bg-mo-neutral-100">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-mo-neutral-800 mb-2">Kansas City Split</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
          Under the current map, Kansas City is largely contained within CD-5. The HB 1 map
          splits the city across three districts (CD-4, CD-5, and CD-6), diluting its
          concentrated voting power. The new CD-5 stretches from a sliver of Kansas City
          eastward through 14 rural counties to Osage County.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-5 rounded-lg bg-mo-neutral-100">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-mo-neutral-800 mb-2">Troost Avenue &amp; Racial History</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
          The new map uses Troost Avenue — Kansas City's historic line of racial segregation
          since the 1920s — as a dividing line between CD-4 and CD-5. Majority-Black neighborhoods
          east of Troost are placed into the rural-stretching CD-5, while predominantly white
          neighborhoods west of Troost go to CD-4. Critics say this reopens long-standing racial
          wounds; supporters say the line follows existing political boundaries.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-5 rounded-lg bg-mo-neutral-100">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-mo-neutral-800 mb-2">Rep. Cleaver's District</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
          Rep. Emanuel Cleaver (D), who has represented CD-5 since 2005 and won reelection
          with over 60% in both 2022 and 2024, now faces a district that leans heavily
          Republican. Four Republican candidates filed for the redrawn seat shortly after
          the map was signed. Inside Elections shifted the seat rating from Solid D to Solid R.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-5 rounded-lg bg-mo-neutral-100">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-mo-neutral-800 mb-2">Rural Communities</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
          The new CD-5 pairs Kansas City urban voters with rural voters across 14 counties
          who have different policy priorities — agriculture, broadband access, and rural
          healthcare vs. urban transit, housing, and city services. Both communities may
          find their specific concerns diluted in a sprawling, mixed district.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Legal Arguments -->
  <section class="container-site py-8">
    <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-mo-neutral-800 mb-4">Legal Arguments</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 mb-6 max-w-3xl">
      Mid-cycle redistricting raises important constitutional questions. Both sides present
      substantive legal arguments grounded in federal and state law. The Missouri Supreme
      Court settled the state-constitution question in March 2026 by upholding the
      legislature's power to redraw mid-decade. The dispute has since shifted to the
      referendum and the federal courts (<a href="#where-it-stands" class="text-mo-navy underline hover:text-mo-navy-light">see Where It Stands</a>).
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
      <!-- Arguments FOR -->
      <div class="rounded-lg border-2 border-mo-neutral-300 overflow-hidden">
        <div class="bg-mo-neutral-200 px-5 py-3">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-mo-neutral-800">Arguments Supporting the New Map</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="p-5 space-y-4">
          <div>
            <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-mo-neutral-700 mb-1">Constitutional Authority</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
              The U.S. Constitution's Elections Clause (Art. I, Sec. 4) grants state legislatures
              broad authority over congressional election procedures. Neither federal law nor the
              Constitution limits redistricting to once per decade.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-mo-neutral-700 mb-1">Supreme Court Precedent</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
              In <em>LULAC v. Perry</em> (2006), the U.S. Supreme Court explicitly ruled that
              mid-decade redistricting is not per se unconstitutional. Texas's 2003 mid-cycle
              redistricting was upheld. States may redistrict as often as they choose.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-mo-neutral-700 mb-1">Governor's Justification</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
              Gov. Kehoe stated the existing map "may be vulnerable to a legal challenge under
              the Voting Rights Act and the Fourteenth Amendment, due to a lack of compactness
              in certain districts."
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-mo-neutral-700 mb-1">State Constitution</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
              Supporters argue that Missouri's once-per-decade redistricting language applies only
              to state legislative districts, not congressional districts, leaving the legislature
              free to redraw congressional maps at any time.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Arguments AGAINST -->
      <div class="rounded-lg border-2 border-mo-neutral-300 overflow-hidden">
        <div class="bg-mo-neutral-200 px-5 py-3">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-mo-neutral-800">Arguments Opposing the New Map</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="p-5 space-y-4">
          <div>
            <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-mo-neutral-700 mb-1">Missouri Constitution</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
              The ACLU and Campaign Legal Center argue the Missouri Constitution permits congressional
              redistricting only once per decade following the decennial census. They also claim
              the new districts violate the state's compactness requirement (Art. III, Sec. 45),
              which requires districts be "as compact as may be."
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-mo-neutral-700 mb-1">Voting Rights Act</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
              Six of nine House members targeted by Republican redistricting efforts nationally
              are Black or Latino. Critics argue the maps dilute minority voting power in
              potential violation of Section 2 of the Voting Rights Act.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-mo-neutral-700 mb-1">Partisan Gerrymandering</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
              Opponents characterize the map as a partisan gerrymander drawn purely to maximize
              Republican seats with no legitimate justification — no new census data, no court
              order, no significant population shifts since 2020.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-mo-neutral-700 mb-1">Historical Norms</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
              Before Texas in 2003, no state had attempted mid-decade redistricting for partisan
              purposes since the Supreme Court's "one person, one vote" decisions. Critics warn
              that normalizing mid-cycle redraws invites an escalating cycle of retaliatory
              gerrymandering.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Where It Stands -->
  <section id="where-it-stands" class="container-site py-8">
    <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-mo-neutral-800 mb-2">Where It Stands</h2>
    <p class="text-xs text-mo-neutral-500 italic mb-4">Last updated September 23, 2026.</p>

    <div class="max-w-3xl space-y-6">
      <!-- State constitutional challenge — resolved -->
      <div class="p-5 rounded-lg border border-mo-neutral-200 bg-white">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-mo-neutral-800 mb-2">State Constitutional Challenge: Resolved</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
          Voters represented by the ACLU, ACLU of Missouri, and Campaign Legal Center argued that
          the Missouri Constitution allows congressional redistricting only once a decade. On
          <strong>March 24, 2026</strong>, the Missouri Supreme Court disagreed in
          <em>Luther v. Hoskins</em> (4-3, opinion by Judge Zel Fischer). It held that
          Article III, Section 45 requires lawmakers to redraw districts after each census
          but does not prohibit redrawing them at other times. That ended the argument that
          mid-decade redistricting is unconstitutional under state law.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Referendum -->
      <div class="p-5 rounded-lg border border-mo-neutral-200 bg-white">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-mo-neutral-800 mb-2">Citizen Referendum: On the November Ballot as Proposition A</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
          The citizen group <strong>People Not Politicians</strong> submitted about 305,000
          signatures in December 2025 to force a statewide vote on HB 1. Secretary of State
          Denny Hoskins (R) refused to certify the petition. He argued that signatures gathered
          before he approved the petition form on October 14, 2025 were invalid, and that
          congressional maps cannot be put to a referendum at all. Two Cole County circuit
          judges agreed with him in August 2026.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed mt-2">
          On <strong>September 3, 2026</strong>, the Missouri Supreme Court unanimously reversed
          them in <em>von Glahn v. Hoskins</em> (opinion by Judge Ginger Gooch). It held that the
          constitution's referendum power over "any act of the general assembly" includes
          congressional redistricting, and that the petition was "legal, sufficient, and
          timely." A qualifying referendum suspends the law it targets, so the court concluded
          that the 2025 map "is not the law and has never been the law." It ordered the
          2022 map used in November. Candidates nominated in the August primary remain on the ballot.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed mt-2">
          <strong>On the ballot:</strong> A <em>Yes</em> vote on Proposition A approves the
          2025 map. A <em>No</em> vote rejects it and keeps the 2022 map.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Federal litigation -->
      <div class="p-5 rounded-lg border border-mo-neutral-200 bg-white">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-mo-neutral-800 mb-2">Federal Court Fight: Unresolved</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
          The August primary was held under the 2025 map. After the state ruling, Republican
          nominee Rick Brattin, Rep. Bob Onder, and two voters sued in federal court. They
          argued that switching to the 2022 map after the primary violates the 14th
          Amendment's Equal Protection Clause and Article I, Section 2 of the U.S. Constitution.
          Voters would face general-election nominees chosen in different districts.
        </p>
        <ul class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed mt-2 list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
          <li>
            <strong>Sept 8:</strong> Justice Brett Kavanaugh denied Secretary Hoskins' request
            to pause the Missouri Supreme Court ruling. The same day, Chief U.S. District Judge
            Stephen Clark issued a temporary restraining order requiring the 2025 map.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Sept 10:</strong> The full U.S. Supreme Court stayed Judge Clark's order in
            a brief unsigned order with no noted dissents. People Not Politicians had argued
            that a single judge could not issue the order because such challenges require a
            three-judge court.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Sept 21:</strong> A unanimous 8th Circuit panel held that "only use of the
            2025 map complies with the Constitution and federal law." It noted that Missourians
            had already filed, campaigned, and voted under that map. The court directed entry
            of a permanent order but paused it until 5 p.m. on September 28.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Sept 22:</strong> People Not Politicians asked the U.S. Supreme Court to
            clarify that its September 10 order still controls, or to block the 8th Circuit
            ruling. They invoked the <em>Purcell</em> principle, which holds that courts should
            not change election rules close to an election. Justice Kavanaugh ordered a response
            by September 23.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed mt-2">
          Ballots for military and overseas voters were sent on September 19, and absentee
          ballots using the 2022 districts began going out on September 23. If the Supreme
          Court does not act before the stay expires, the 8th Circuit's order could force
          Missouri to reissue ballots.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Each side's position on the current dispute -->
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <div class="p-5 rounded-lg bg-mo-neutral-100">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-mo-neutral-800 mb-2 text-sm">Case for Using the 2025 Map</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
            The primary was lawfully held under the 2025 districts, and nominees were chosen
            there. Holding the general election under different lines would pair voters with
            nominees they had no chance to choose. Attorney General Catherine Hanaway called
            the state ruling "a full-fledged constitutional crisis." Supporters also argue
            that the U.S. Constitution assigns congressional districting to the legislature.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-5 rounded-lg bg-mo-neutral-100">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-mo-neutral-800 mb-2 text-sm">Case for Using the 2022 Map</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-mo-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
            Missouri's highest court has ruled that the valid referendum suspended the 2025 map
            before the primary, so the map never took legal effect. The U.S. Supreme Court has
            twice left that outcome in place. Switching maps again after ballots are printed
            and mailed would cause the kind of voter confusion <em>Purcell</em> is meant to prevent.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

